[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/h6HmRwJi/KoBZL38y"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/anorakii"]
[FEN "8/8/8/p7/8/1k6/3K4/8 w - - 0 17"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2022.10.14"]
[UTCTime "14:08:26"]

{ Unfortunately for the strong side, standing in the pawn's path is not the only way for the defender to draw this ending. The vicinity of the edge to the board can also dramatically restrict the mobility of the stronger side's king. } { [%csl Gc2,Gc1,Gb3][%cal Ga4a1] }
17. Kc1 { [%csl Ga1][%cal Gc1a1] } 17... Ka2 { The only move to prevent white getting in front of the pawn, which would draw as seen in (1.14) } { [%csl Gb1,Ga1][%cal Gb1a1] } (17... a4 18. Kb1 Kb4 19. Ka2) 18. Kc2 { Now white will not allow the king out of the corner. } { [%csl Gc1][%cal Gc1c2] } 18... a4 19. Kc1 (19. Kc3) 19... a3 20. Kc2 Ka1 21. Kc1 a2 { We draw by stalemate or three-fold repetition. Thus we can see the key squares for the defender are c1 and c2. } { [%csl Ga1] } *


