[UTCTime "00:32:49"]

6... Kf8 (6... Kh8 7. Ke6 Kg8 8. Ke7+ Kh8 9. Kf7 Bb2 10. g7+ { We can see that Kh8 is the only way to draw. }) 7. Ke6 Bb2 { And now black simply waits by shuffling the bishop, there is no progress possible for white. Thus, rook + knight pawns are a draw when the bishop is on the long diagonal. } *


[Event "100 Endgames You Must Know by Jesus de la Villa: Ending 42: Pawns on the 5th rank or behind (opposite bishops) (9.4)"]
[Date "2022.12.13"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "100 Endgames You Must Know by Jesus de la Villa"]
[ChapterName "Ending 42: Pawns on the 5th rank or behind (opposite bishops) (9.4)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/h6HmRwJi/6XunDxt0"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/anorakii"]
[FEN "8/4k3/8/4PP2/4K3/1b6/3B4/8 w - - 6 8"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2022.12.13"]
[UTCTime "00:36:30"]

{ In the previous ending, we saw that 6th rank pawns are a win unless they are rook and knight pawns. However, here we will see that 5th rank pawns are not enough to win. }
8. Bg5+ { First, we force the king to choose which side to move to. There are two checks to choose from, and a big difference. White will push the e-pawn, and the bishop must control both squares in front of the pawns. } (8. Bb4+ Kf7 9. Kd4 (9. Be1 { But white can still return to the winning plan. } 9... Ke7 10. Bh4+) 9... Bc2 { And this is the crux of the choice between bishop checks, now the pawns are disrupted and black can blockade. } 10. f6 Ke6) 8... Kd7 (8... Kf7 9. Kd4 { Kf7 is the wrong choice, now we outflank to control promotion. }) 9. Kf4 { We move to outflank on the other side, to secure the e6-square from f6. } 9... Ba2 10. Bh4 Bf7 11. Kg5 Ke7 12. Kh6+ Kd7 13. Kg7 Bd5 14. Kf6 Bb3 15. e6+ Ke8 16. Ke5 { And now we convert to ending 41, two 6th rank pawns are a win. But there is a defensive procedure to prevent this conversion! } *


[Event "100 Endgames You Must Know by Jesus de la Villa: Ending 42: Pawns on the 5th rank or behind (opposite bishops) (9.5)"]
[Date "2022.12.13"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "100 Endgames You Must Know by Jesus de la Villa"]
[ChapterName "Ending 42: Pawns on the 5th rank or behind (opposite bishops) (9.5)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/h6HmRwJi/79O05plr"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/anorakii"]
[FEN "2b5/4k3/8/4PP2/4K3/8/3B4/8 w - - 2 16"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2022.12.13"]
[UTCTime "00:45:19"]

{ From c8 (or d7), the bishop attacks the f5 pawn, thus hindering outflanking maneuvers and allows a sacrifice against an eventual e5-e6. }
16. Bg5+ { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge5e6,Gc8e6] } 16... Kf7 { [%csl Gd5,Gf5][%cal Ge4d5] } 17. Kf4 (17. f6 Be6) 17... Bd7 { And there is no progress to be made for white. We must maintain a defense of our f5 pawn, and if we push either pawn, it allows a blockade (after f6) or a sacrifice (after e6). } *


[Event "100 Endgames You Must Know by Jesus de la Villa: Ending 42: Pawns on the 4th rank (opposite bishops) (9.6)"]
[Date "2022.12.13"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "100 Endgames You Must Know by Jesus de la Villa"]
[ChapterName "Ending 42: Pawns on the 4th rank (opposite bishops) (9.6)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/h6HmRwJi/7SkLnCdc"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/anorakii"]
[FEN "8/8/3k4/8/3PP3/4K3/8/4Bb2 b - - 6 18"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2022.12.13"]
[UTCTime "00:48:38"]

{ Here the defender has more options. The correct defensive position can be reached when pawns are on the 4th rank or 5th. }
18... Bc4 { Only move, black must reach the key squares. } { [%csl Gf7,Gg8] } 19. Bg3+ Kc6 (19... Ke6 20. Bf4 Ba2 { [%csl Gf7,Gg8] } 21. Kd2 Bb3 22. Kc3 Ba2 23. Kb4 Bb1 24. d5+ Kf6 25. e5+ Ke7 26. Kb5 Bf5 27. Kc6 Bd7+ 28. Kc7 Bh3 29. Bg5+ { And thus, we can see that Ke6 loses, as the bishop cannot get to the key squares, allowing for the king to outflank. }) 20. Kf4 Bf7 21. Ke5 Kd7 22. d5 Bg8 23. Kd4 Bf7 24. e5 Bg8 { And we can see that white will never be able to progress from here without allowing the blockade or sacrifice. } *


