{ The defensive procedure is simple, the king waits while the bishop shuffles maintaining control of the pawn supported by the king (c-pawn in this example). }
1. Bb3 Be5 2. Kb6 Bd6 3. Kb5 Bc7 4. Kc4 Bd6 5. Kd5 Bc7 6. Ke4 Bd6 7. Kf5 Bc7 8. Kf6 Bd6 9. Kf7 Bc5 { And now we switch to defend e7, simple draw. } *


